  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 253 PM CDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nThe main challenges in this forecast package will continue to \nrevolve around the chances for severe weather and locally heavy \nrainfall the remainder of today into tonight and Friday morning. Mid \nafternoon satellite/radar imagery indicate ongoing active weather \nacross this forecast area. Showers and thunderstorms have continued \nto evolve across northeast SD/west central MN early this afternoon \nwhile the area of showers/storms the moved across south central SD \nthrough the first half of the day have shifted into southeast SD. \ndevelopment of more robust convection has taken place over the last \nhour or so across parts of far south central SD, south of the I-90 \ncorridor. This activity appears to be shifting east-southeast and \nmay clip our far south/southwest zones this afternoon where a Severe \nThunderstorm Watch has been issued until 9 PM CDT tonight.\n\nThe disturbance partly responsible for this active pattern today is \nin the form of a mid level shortwave trough that is working into \nwestern SD this afternoon. The lead shortwave ahead of this feature \nwas responsible for this mornings activity has shifting east and \nsoutheast. This upper level disturbance is encountering a modestly \nunstable air mass with MLCAPE values ranging between 2000-3000 J/kg. \nSo, there's enough instability for storms to continue. However, deep \nlayer shear remains mostly lacking across most of our CWA, outside \nof far southern zones and perhaps near the ND/SD border region where \n0-6 KM bulk shear values range between 25-30 kts. Weak flow in the \nlow to mid levels of the atmosphere have played havoc on the \nevolution of convection and thus hi-res guidance has had a tough \ntime discerning what comes of all of this. Also, outflow boundaries \ncreated by existing or earlier convection is also playing or will \nplay a role in future storms as the latter half of the afternoon and \nevening wear on. So, best chances for severe weather for the \nremainder of this event the rest of today and tonight will be across \nparts of central and south central SD. Initial discrete cells will \nbe capable of large hail but with time, storms will evolve into more \nclusters or linear mode and the threat will become damaging winds. \nPerhaps the more widespread threat across most of the forecast area \nwill be locally heavy rainfall. A Slight Risk for Excessive Rainfall \nremains posted for most of the forecast area with our eastern half \nof zones having the best chance for seeing 1-2 inches of rainfall. \nSome localized amounts of 3 inches or more will be possible, \nespecially from parts of west central MN into east central SD south \nand west into sections of the James Valley and far south central SD. \nThese area will have the best chance to see localized flash flooding \nwith any storms. The slow moving nature of this convection will only \nhelp contribute further to the flooding possibilities.\n\nThe upper level disturbance shifts south and east of the area on \nFriday. Lingering rains across our eastern zones will diminish by \nmidday leaving drier condition to settle in during the afternoon. \nHigh pressure building in from the north will lead to breezy north \nto northeast winds on Friday. Temperatures will remain close to \nnormal if not slightly below normal(especially eastern zones) on \nFriday. Upper ridging is progged to build into the region through \nthe course of the upcoming weekend. This will bring back the heat to \nthe region, especially across central SD. Temperature progs indicate \nthose locations will be back into the 90s to around 100 degrees \nSaturday into Sunday and to a certain extent on Monday. Southerly \nflow returns and with a tighter gradient developing by late in the \nweekend and a drier air mass, elevated fire weather conditions are \nexpected to develop across parts of central and north central SD. \nAfter another fropa early next week, a cooler than normal air mass \nwill infiltrate the area by Tuesday through the middle of next week.",
